ing major population centers annually has more than doubled since the 1950s.\n- This option suggests that the number of tornadoes hitting major population centers has increased. If this is true, it would support the meteorologist's argument that the increase in reported tornadoes is due to better detection rather than an actual increase in the number of tornadoes. Therefore, this option supports the meteorologist's argument.\n\nC) The geographic range in which tornadoes are most prevalent has remained roughly constant since the 1950s.\n- This option suggests that the geographic range of tornadoes has not changed. If this is true, it would not provide any support for the meteorologist's argument. Therefore, this option does not support the meteorologist's argument.\n\nD) The number of large and medium-sized tornadoes reported annually has remained roughly constant since the 1950s.\n- This option suggests that the number of large and medium-sized tornadoes has not changed. If this is true, it would support the meteorologist's argument that the increase in reported tornadoes is due to better detection rather than an actual increase in the number of tornadoes. Therefore, this option supports the meteorologist's argument.\n\nBased on the analysis, options B and D both support the meteorologist's argument. Therefore, my final option is: "]]
