some second-grade students are children of migrant workers, so it's possible that all second-grade students are not children of migrant workers, but it's not guaranteed.\n\n**Option B: Some children of migrant workers are third grade students.**.\n- This statement is true. We know that some first-grade students are the children of migrant workers, and since some of these students are in the third grade, it follows that some children of migrant workers are indeed third-grade students.\n\n**Option C: Some students with household registration in this city are third-grade students.**.\n- This statement is true. We know that all third-grade students have a household registration in S City, so it's clear that some students with household registration in this city are third-grade students.\n\n**Option D: Some first-grade students are not children of migrant workers.**.\n- This statement is true. We know that some first-grade students are the children of migrant workers, but it's also true that some first-grade students are not children of migrant workers. Therefore, my final option is: "]]
