messages:  [['Please solve the logical reasoning problem.\nGiven a context and a question, answer with the correct option: A, B, C or D. Please reason step by step.\n####\n# Context:\nObligatory alternative action refers to a situation in which the actor has committed an illegal act and caused the result, but the result cannot be avoided even if he abides by the law.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, according to the above definition, which of the following is an optional act of obligation?\n# Options:\nA) When a was driving normally, a pedestrian named wang rushed to a from the sidewalk and wang was injured\nB) B drunk driving, pedestrian wang also drunk, from the sidewalk suddenly rushed to b, wang was injured\nC) C driving speeding, pedestrian wang across the road, c too late to brake, wang hit\nD) D overload driving, pedestrian wang across the road, fortunately, ding emergency brake, did not hit wang. I think the correct option is: C\n', "<think>.\nTo solve this logical reasoning problem, we need to identify the situation that best fits the definition of an obligatory alternative action. According to the definition, an obligatory alternative action occurs when the actor has committed an illegal act and caused the result, but the result cannot be avoided even if he abides by the law.\n\nLet's analyze each option:.\n\nA) When a was driving normally, a pedestrian named wang rushed to a from the sidewalk and wang was injured.\n- In this case, there is no illegal act committed by the actor (a). Wang's injury is not caused by any illegal act. Therefore, this does not meet the criteria for an obligatory alternative action.\n\nB) B drunk driving, pedestrian wang also drunk, from the sidewalk suddenly rushed to b, wang was injured.\n- In this case, both the actor (B) and the pedestrian (Wang) are drunk. The drunk driving by B is an illegal act, and the resulting injury to Wang is caused by this illegal act. However, Wang's injury is not unavoidable even if B had not been drunk. Therefore, this does not meet the criteria for an obligatory alternative action.\n\nC) C driving speeding, pedestrian wang across the road, c too late to brake, wang hit.\n- In this case, the actor (C) is driving at a high speed, which is an illegal act. The resulting injury to Wang is caused by this illegal act. However, Wang's injury is unavoidable even if C had not been speeding. Therefore, this does not meet the criteria for an obligatory alternative action.\n\nD) D overload driving, pedestrian wang across the road, fortunately, ding emergency brake, did not hit wang.\n- In this case, the actor (D) is overloaded, which is an illegal act. The resulting injury to Wang is caused by this illegal act. However, Wang's injury is unavoidable even if D had not been overloaded. Therefore, this meets the criteria for an obligatory alternative action. Therefore, my final option is: "]]
