 based on their political affiliations.\n\nOption B: Almost all of the officials who have served in city government for any length of time are appointees of mayor Bixby. This option does not weaken the argument because it does not provide any information about the task force's targeting of officials based on their political affiliations.\n\nOption C: Former mayor Bixby did not publicly oppose mayor Stephens' establishment of the anticorruption task force. This option does not weaken the argument because it does not provide any information about the task force's targeting of officials based on their political affiliations.\n\nOption D: All of the members of the anticorruption task force had other jobs in city government before the task force was formed. This option does not weaken the argument because it does not provide any information about the task force's targeting of officials based on their political affiliations.\n\nBased on the analysis, the option that most weakens the editorial's argument is Option A, as it suggests that the task force is effective in reducing corruption, which weakens the argument that the task force is targeting officials based on their political affiliations.\n. Therefore, my final option is: "]]
