mented.\n- This statement is not necessarily true. The third statement only says that equalizing the rich and the poor is necessary for absolute fairness, but it does not imply that absolute fairness must be implemented. It could be that other forms of fairness are used instead.\n\n**Option D:** Can not implement absolute fairness.\n- This statement is true. The second statement says that if distribution according to work is implemented, there will be inequality between the rich and the poor. The third statement says that only by equalizing the rich and the poor can absolute fairness be implemented. Since the second statement shows that inequality will occur with distribution according to work, it means that absolute fairness cannot be implemented.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is:. Therefore, my final option is: "]]
