will try their best not to attract the attention of the police.\n- This option implies that swindlers are cautious about being caught. While this might be true, it does not directly address the issue of the swindlers' intelligence or professionalism. Therefore, this option does not strongly refute the conclusion.\n\nC) If a deception was useless, the swindler would have eliminated it long ago.\n- This option suggests that swindlers would eliminate useless deceptions. However, the context does not provide any information about the swindlers' ability to evaluate the effectiveness of their deceptions. Therefore, this option does not directly refute the conclusion.\n\nD) Swindlers use such text messages to catch people who may be fooled, and they want to filter out smart people in the first place.\n- This option suggests that swindlers are aware of the intelligence level of potential victims and are targeting those who are more likely to be fooled. This directly refutes the conclusion that swindlers are too stupid and unprofessional because it implies that they are intentionally targeting smart people. Therefore, my final option is: "]]
